WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.923 --> 00:00:08.235
(bright music)

00:00:08.235 --> 00:00:09.720
(laughter)

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.319
- I definitely look up to my sister.

00:00:11.319 --> 00:00:14.456
She has a lot of great
things that I can learn from

00:00:14.456 --> 00:00:18.182
and I think she's an awesome older sister.

00:00:18.182 --> 00:00:19.677
- Ow.

00:00:19.677 --> 00:00:20.842
Thank you, meimei.

00:00:20.842 --> 00:00:22.621
Kimberly is my best friend.

00:00:22.621 --> 00:00:25.513
She's never going to be replaced.

00:00:25.513 --> 00:00:28.285
- [Kimberly] I like to do martial arts

00:00:28.285 --> 00:00:31.145
and then I also like to do basketball.

00:00:31.145 --> 00:00:32.978
- I like to do archery

00:00:35.273 --> 00:00:36.523
and play piano.

00:00:37.555 --> 00:00:39.789
I have a lot of favorite subjects.

00:00:39.789 --> 00:00:41.795
I like math, and English,

00:00:41.795 --> 00:00:44.878
and social studies, and
science and technology.

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:47.446
- I like technology the best.

00:00:47.446 --> 00:00:49.880
These are some of the programs that I did,

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:52.248
but I also really like science.

00:00:52.248 --> 00:00:54.531
- I like challenges.

00:00:54.531 --> 00:00:56.406
We saw some videos

00:00:56.406 --> 00:00:59.779
of people doing weather
balloon launches online.

00:00:59.779 --> 00:01:01.313
- We thought that it would be a really fun

00:01:01.313 --> 00:01:03.086
family project to do.

00:01:03.086 --> 00:01:05.669
(bright music)

00:01:10.872 --> 00:01:13.944
- [Rebecca] A weather balloon is basically

00:01:13.944 --> 00:01:16.056
just a very large balloon.

00:01:16.056 --> 00:01:18.693
- They're used to see what
the weather is going to be.

00:01:18.693 --> 00:01:20.539
- So they send up different
instruments attached

00:01:20.539 --> 00:01:22.363
to the bottom of the weather balloons.

00:01:22.363 --> 00:01:24.644
- And they'll transmit the data and say,

00:01:24.644 --> 00:01:26.992
"Oh it's gonna be partly cloudy."

00:01:26.992 --> 00:01:29.659
- There are 16,000 weather
balloons launched every day

00:01:29.659 --> 00:01:30.809
around the world.

00:01:30.809 --> 00:01:33.190
The weather companies, the forecasters

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:35.387
only recover 30 percent of them.

00:01:35.387 --> 00:01:36.742
The other 70 percent.

00:01:36.742 --> 00:01:38.511
- Are still floating in space.

00:01:38.511 --> 00:01:39.344
- No they're not.

00:01:39.344 --> 00:01:40.177
- Well.

00:01:40.177 --> 00:01:41.286
- They're still somewhere.

00:01:41.286 --> 00:01:43.088
- They're somewhere in the air.

00:01:43.088 --> 00:01:44.400
- Try not to interrupt me.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.983
(bright music)

00:01:53.338 --> 00:01:55.142
- [Kimberly] This is
our Loki Lego Launcher.

00:01:55.142 --> 00:01:58.907
The first spacecraft in the
Yeungstuff Space Program.

00:01:58.907 --> 00:02:02.072
- For the first launch, we
didn't really have a goal.

00:02:02.072 --> 00:02:05.122
- We just wanted to get it off the ground.

00:02:05.122 --> 00:02:08.866
We didn't really understand
as much science then.

00:02:08.866 --> 00:02:11.181
We were kind of just
letting go of it hoping

00:02:11.181 --> 00:02:12.760
that it went well.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:14.552
- The calculations were right.

00:02:14.552 --> 00:02:18.413
- We call it the Loki Lego
Launcher because Loki is our cat.

00:02:18.413 --> 00:02:22.210
Our flight computer keeps
track of a number of things,

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:25.837
including altitude,
latitude, longitude, speed,

00:02:25.837 --> 00:02:29.154
temperature, pressure,
voltage, current and power.

00:02:29.154 --> 00:02:31.725
There is a lot of data
that we can learn from.

00:02:31.725 --> 00:02:34.690
- The first launch went to 78,000 feet.

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:36.877
The images were awesome.

00:02:36.877 --> 00:02:39.138
When we first saw them we screamed.

00:02:39.138 --> 00:02:40.994
It's just the blackness of space

00:02:40.994 --> 00:02:44.013
and then there's Loki sitting
there in the middle of it

00:02:44.013 --> 00:02:45.348
right on the horizon.

00:02:45.348 --> 00:02:47.931
(bright music)

00:02:54.002 --> 00:02:54.835
The first media that kind of
wrote an article about us

00:02:54.835 --> 00:02:58.918
was a local tech news
website called The Geekwire

00:03:02.088 --> 00:03:06.088
and then someone from the
Washington Post saw it

00:03:07.132 --> 00:03:08.765
and so they wrote an article about it

00:03:08.765 --> 00:03:10.258
and then other people saw it

00:03:10.258 --> 00:03:12.018
and they wrote articles about it.

00:03:12.018 --> 00:03:13.064
- [Man] You're going to have

00:03:13.064 --> 00:03:14.472
to bring the Loki Lego Launcher.

00:03:14.472 --> 00:03:15.889
- No, no, no way!

00:03:17.772 --> 00:03:19.020
Seriously?

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:20.854
- [Man] You're going
to meet the president.

00:03:20.854 --> 00:03:22.849
- Our parents told us that we were going

00:03:22.849 --> 00:03:24.576
to the White House science fair.

00:03:24.576 --> 00:03:27.159
(bright music)

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:30.135
(applause)

00:03:30.135 --> 00:03:31.116
- Well hello everybody.

00:03:31.116 --> 00:03:32.321
Welcome to the White House.

00:03:32.321 --> 00:03:34.092
- When he actually
walked through the doors,

00:03:34.092 --> 00:03:38.325
we were all in complete
awe and admiration.

00:03:38.325 --> 00:03:39.201
- Hey guys.

00:03:39.201 --> 00:03:42.186
- [Rebecca] Because
this is President Obama.

00:03:42.186 --> 00:03:43.978
- He was really easy to talk to

00:03:43.978 --> 00:03:45.537
and he was really friendly.

00:03:45.537 --> 00:03:47.031
- What do you guys have?

00:03:47.031 --> 00:03:50.039
- So we built a spacecraft
called the Loki Lego Launcher

00:03:50.039 --> 00:03:52.695
and we sent it up to 78,000 feet.

00:03:52.695 --> 00:03:54.647
- [President Obama] That's crazy.

00:03:54.647 --> 00:03:58.401
- He talks to all of
these famous world leaders

00:03:58.401 --> 00:04:01.900
and makes decisions that
affect the fate of our country

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:04.727
and we're just 9 and 11 year old girls

00:04:04.727 --> 00:04:06.894
who did a science project.

00:04:08.577 --> 00:04:11.073
This is a rough drawing
of the flight computer

00:04:11.073 --> 00:04:13.655
and we'll probably put the VI sensor here.

00:04:13.655 --> 00:04:15.564
(bright music)

00:04:15.564 --> 00:04:17.931
I am making some changes
to our original design

00:04:17.931 --> 00:04:20.064
because we're adding some new components

00:04:20.064 --> 00:04:21.731
and taking off some.

00:04:27.639 --> 00:04:31.018
- On the second launch
we added a bigger balloon

00:04:31.018 --> 00:04:33.631
because we wanted it to go higher

00:04:33.631 --> 00:04:36.663
and we also wanted it to go faster.

00:04:36.663 --> 00:04:40.833
We added an APRS radio
tracker which sent us location

00:04:40.833 --> 00:04:43.713
and altitude data in almost real time.

00:04:43.713 --> 00:04:46.532
- However, the APRS might
not be very accurate

00:04:46.532 --> 00:04:49.412
at ground level, so having
the GPS unit as well

00:04:49.412 --> 00:04:51.204
is very helpful.

00:04:51.204 --> 00:04:56.160
We rewired the voltage/current/power
sensor so it would

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:59.456
connect to a solar panel that
we installed on our spacecraft

00:04:59.456 --> 00:05:03.787
to measure how much current
it was getting from the sun

00:05:03.787 --> 00:05:07.945
and we had a hypothesis that
as our spacecraft went higher,

00:05:07.945 --> 00:05:10.997
we would have more solar
current because we thought

00:05:10.997 --> 00:05:13.431
that since there's less
particles in the air

00:05:13.431 --> 00:05:15.339
it wouldn't block the sun's rays.

00:05:15.339 --> 00:05:16.930
(drill buzzing)

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:19.693
The last thing that we changed
was our Lego minifigure.

00:05:19.693 --> 00:05:22.509
We thought she would be a good role model

00:05:22.509 --> 00:05:25.741
because she's a girl who's
strong and she's brave

00:05:25.741 --> 00:05:28.962
and she doesn't give up
even when things are hard.

00:05:28.962 --> 00:05:31.545
(bright music)

00:05:33.346 --> 00:05:36.846
- [Kimberly] Soon, it was time for launch.

00:05:39.768 --> 00:05:40.601
- 3,2,1

00:05:42.434 --> 00:05:43.267
Lift off!

00:05:47.352 --> 00:05:49.935
(bright music)

00:05:53.901 --> 00:05:57.858
- We couldn't believe that it
actually went 101,000 feet.

00:05:57.858 --> 00:05:58.936
I think we screamed again.

00:05:58.936 --> 00:05:59.968
(screaming)

00:05:59.968 --> 00:06:01.698
- Our current data was really interesting

00:06:01.698 --> 00:06:04.280
because our solar panel
was producing more current

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:06.328
as the altitude was going up

00:06:06.328 --> 00:06:09.592
which indicated that our
hypothesis was correct.

00:06:09.592 --> 00:06:11.704
- Getting something correct,

00:06:11.704 --> 00:06:13.709
especially when you don't know
that much about the subject,

00:06:13.709 --> 00:06:15.209
always feels good.

00:06:18.506 --> 00:06:21.506
I hope that more kids, especially girls,

00:06:21.506 --> 00:06:25.592
follow paths and do
projects like ours in STEM

00:06:25.592 --> 00:06:28.888
and I hope that our projects teach them

00:06:28.888 --> 00:06:31.021
that they can do whatever they want

00:06:31.021 --> 00:06:32.728
if they put their heart into it.

00:06:32.728 --> 00:06:37.229
- They don't have to stick with
whatever is girly or boyish.

00:06:37.229 --> 00:06:40.896
Girls are awesome and
girls can do anything.

